Create Your Own Ribbon Tab/Context Menu in the Frame
You can create your own ribbon tab/context menu in the Frame.
To create a user-defined ribbon tab or a user-defined context menu with your own, frequently used, ribbon
commands or context menu commands respectively:
• Click Configure User-defined Ribbon Tab or Configure User-defined Context Menu on the Frame
ribbon tab of the Frame for which you want to create the user-defined ribbon tab or user-defined
context menu.
If a user-defined ribbon tab exists, the button on the Frame ribbon tab is highlighted.
Note
To create a user-defined ribbon tab in a Frame, which you inserted into another Frame, deactivate the
command Inherit, so that it does not show a check mark to the left.
• Type in the Title of the user-defined ribbon tab with which Plant Simulation shows the user-defined
ribbon tab. We typed in My Ribbon Tab.
• To show the user-defined ribbon tab for the Frame, for which you defined it, select Active.
• Type in the name of the command that Plant Simulation shows on the user-defined ribbon tab in the
Frame. Type an & (ampersand) in front of a letter to make this letter the access key. You can select this
Create Your Own Ribbon Tab/Context Menu in the Frame
10-114 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

ribbon command by holding down Alt, by pressing the predefined key combination U and T, and by the
pressing that letter. The built-in Plant Simulation access keys take precedence over any access keys you
define!
You can also type in a formula as a ribbon command or a context menu command. A formula is
designated by a leading question mark. When you type in ?Method1 for example, the method named
Method1 will be called. The return value of this method has to be of data type string. This way you can
switch between different texts, for example between Activate and Deactivate and you can translate the
ribbon or context menu commands into different languages. If the methods return an empty string "",
Plant Simulation hides the respective ribbon or context menu command.
Note
You can use any formulas, even for example a method call with parameters, such as ?Method1(42)
or a table access, such as ?DataTable[1,3].
• Type in the Methods, which the ribbon/context menu commands execute into the text boxes below
Method to execute.
If the method expects a parameter, Plant Simulation automatically passes it. To this parameter, which
has to be of data type list, the selected objects, which are located within the same Frame is the Frame
whose ribbon tab or context menu is opened, are passed.
When Methods are called from the user-defined ribbon tab, the anonymous identifier ? (question mark)
points to the Frame in which you selected the command. This way you can access objects within the
respective Frame.
Note
The commands on the user-defined context menu only apply to the selected object(s) within the
Frame for which you defined the context menu.
The Class Library does not show the user-defined context menu.
Create Your Own Ribbon Tab/Context Menu in the Frame
Plant Simulation Help 10-115
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

User-defined ribbon tab of a Frame User-defined context menu of a Frame
• To create the user-defined ribbon tab or the user-defined context menu, click OK. Plant Simulation
shows the ribbon tab to the right of the pre-defined ribbon tabs.
• To open a user-defined dialog from the user-defined ribbon tab:
◦ Type in the name of the command, which opens the user-defined dialog, into the text box below Text
to display.
◦ Click 
  to enter the path to and the name of the Method 
 , which tells Plant Simulation to open the
dialog, into the text box below Method to execute.
Create Your Own Ribbon Tab/Context Menu in the Frame
