Model Transitions Between Frames
One of the main advantages of Plant Simulation is that you can model hierarchically, i.e., you can model
components of your overall plant in a Frame, make sure that these components work by themselves and
then insert them into your overall simulation model.
Model Transitions Between Frames
10-130 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

In this case, you will have to connect the component (Frame) to the material flow objects or Frames that
precede and succeed it in the sequence of stations with the object Interface 
 . The transitions are the
places where MUs move from one Frame to another or from a material flow object to a Frame and vice
versa. Plant Simulation shows if the Interface you inserted into a Frame is connected to another object
with a Connector or if it is not connected. Plant Simulation also recognizes if an Interface is an entrance or
an exit.
If the sub-Frame only contains a single material flow object, you do not have to use Interfaces but can
connect the sub-Frame directly with its predecessor and its successor.
Model Transitions Between Frames
Plant Simulation Help 10-131
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The Tooltip shows the externally connected objects. Plant Simulation does not insert a Connector from the
predecessor and to the successor of the object in the sub-Frame.
Our model looks like this in 3D by default as Graphics > Show Content is activated by default:
Model Transitions Between Frames
10-132 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To only show the sub-Frame, clear Show Content for the Frame in the model NoInterfaces. The model
then looks like this:
Model Transitions Between Frames
Plant Simulation Help 10-133
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

You can insert the Interface into your simulation model from the folder MaterialFlow in the Class Library
or from the toolbar Material Flow in the Toolbox.
To model transitions, click the folder or the toolbar Material Flow and insert as many objects of type
Interface 
  anywhere in the Frame.
• Select the side of the icon of the Frame at which the Interface is to be located: on the Top, on the Right,
on the Bottom, or on the Left of the Frame.
• Type in the maximum number of external connections the Interface may have. Depending on the type,
any number of Interfaces may have more than one predecessor or successor.
The predecessor is the object that is connected to the selected object with a Connector and that is
located in front of it in the sequence of stations in the simulation model.
The successor is the object that is connected to the selected object with a Connector and that is located
after it in the sequence of stations in the simulation model.
Model Transitions Between Frames
10-134 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Type the position of the Interface on the side of the Frame it is inserted into in percent into the text box
Position in %. You can specify a value between 0 and 100 percent. Plant Simulation uses the value you
specify, when you activate File > Preferences > General > Connect Objects Automatically. Connecting
objects automatically only works when the exit of FrameA and the entrance of FrameB are not more
than three pixels apart.
• An Interface object you insert into a Frame and connect with a Connector show its Type in the dialog:
either Entrance or Exit.
• If need be, you can also select an Exit Strategy on the tab Exit.
